# Value of an inverse at a rational place

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.evalAt_inv` (`9baa1fd8-1922-59dd-b731-2eb081e17700`), by
Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `e7128a7d-864f-54ec-a284-b804fe552843`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the
project's sense: a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v \subseteq F$ that contains the image of $K$, is
not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring. Assume $v$ is rational, i.e. the induced map $K \to
\mathcal{O}_v/\mathfrak{m}_v$ into the residue field is surjective; evaluation $v.\mathrm{evalAt}$
is then defined on $\mathcal{O}_v$ by composing the residue map with a chosen set-theoretic inverse
of $K \to \mathcal{O}_v/\mathfrak{m}_v$, and is set to $0$ off $\mathcal{O}_v$. Let $f \in F$ with
$f \neq 0$ and with $\operatorname{ord}_v(f) = 0$, where $\operatorname{ord}_v$ is minus the
logarithm of the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation attached to $v$, so that $f$ has neither a
zero nor a pole at $v$. The conclusion is the equality in $K$
$$v.\mathrm{evalAt}(f^{-1}) = \big(v.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)\big)^{-1}.$$

This is the compatibility of evaluation at a rational place with inversion, for functions that are
units at the place; it belongs to the layer of elementary properties of evaluating functions at
places, underneath the treatment of Weil reciprocity. It is used in the computations with annuli and
parameters, for instance when comparing evaluations of a function and of its residue against powers
of a parameter.

